From: PS4 port
Subject: [PATCH] orbis: no usable segfault context on this kernel

flycast's SIGSEGV handler rewrites fastmem accesses in place, which means reading rip, rsp, r9
and rdi out of the third argument the kernel hands a signal handler. It cannot be done here yet,
and the file that would do it does not even compile:

    core/linux/context.cpp:121:47: error: use of undeclared identifier 'REG_RIP'
    core/linux/context.cpp:122:48: error: use of undeclared identifier 'REG_RSP'
    core/linux/context.cpp:123:47: error: use of undeclared identifier 'REG_R9'
    core/linux/context.cpp:124:48: error: use of undeclared identifier 'REG_RDI'

Two things collide there. The first is that this build lands in context.cpp's `__unix__` arm -
glibc's mcontext_t, gregs[REG_RIP] - even though the target triple is x86_64-pc-freebsd12 and
clang really does predefine __FreeBSD__ for it. The SDK's own libc++ takes it back:

    openorbis/include/c++/v1/__config:12: #undef __FreeBSD__

and context.cpp is a C++ file that has already pulled in libc++ headers by the time it tests the
macro. The second is that the arm it lands in does not compile anyway: the SDK ships musl's
<bits/signal.h>, whose REG_RIP and friends are behind #ifdef _GNU_SOURCE, and this build defines
_BSD_SOURCE instead. The FreeBSD arm above it would not have compiled either - that same
mcontext_t has gregs[23] and no mc_rip to name.

So the declared layout is Linux's and the kernel is FreeBSD's, and the two are already known not
to agree. orbis-compat's crash handler dumps thirty-two words of this exact pointer on hardware
and records that NONE of them was a text address, which is why it reaches for backtrace() to find
the faulting instruction instead. Until that offset is measured and written down, naming a field
here would be a guess dressed up as a register.

This is the same shape as play/0004: a platform where the fault handler cannot be given what it
exists to read, and the honest answer is to turn off the one thing that reads it rather than to
feed it something plausible. context_from_segfault/context_to_segfault become documented no-ops,
and the caller stops asking - a zeroed host_context_t handed to sh4Dynarec->rewrite() would be
worse than no fastmem, because it would patch code at pc 0.

What is lost is the fastmem fixup. What is kept is the rest of the handler: the net-rollback RAM
watcher, the RAM and VRAM write protection and the FPCB jump-table lock all key on si->si_addr
alone and are untouched.

diff --git a/core/linux/common.cpp b/core/linux/common.cpp
index 9e20784..ad53605 100644
--- a/core/linux/common.cpp
+++ b/core/linux/common.cpp
@@ -56,6 +56,7 @@ void fault_handler(int sn, siginfo_t * si, void *segfault_ctx)
 	if (addrspace::bm_lockedWrite((u8*)si->si_addr))
 		return;
 
+#ifndef __ORBIS__
 	// fast mem access rewriting
 	host_context_t ctx;
 	context_from_segfault(&ctx, segfault_ctx);
@@ -66,6 +67,15 @@ void fault_handler(int sn, siginfo_t * si, void *segfault_ctx)
 	}
 
 	ERROR_LOG(COMMON, "SIGSEGV @ %p invalid access to %p", (void *)ctx.pc, si->si_addr);
+#else
+	// ⚠ NO REWRITE HERE, BECAUSE THERE IS NO CONTEXT TO REWRITE FROM. core/linux/context.cpp's
+	// __ORBIS__ arm says it in full: this SDK declares musl's mcontext_t while the kernel
+	// passes FreeBSD's, and the offset of rip in what actually arrives has been dumped on
+	// hardware without being identified. Everything above this point still works - the
+	// rollback watcher, the RAM and VRAM write protection and the FPCB lock all key on
+	// si->si_addr alone - so what is lost is the fastmem fixup, not fault handling.
+	ERROR_LOG(COMMON, "SIGSEGV invalid access to %p", si->si_addr);
+#endif
 #endif
 
 #ifdef __SWITCH__
diff --git a/core/linux/context.cpp b/core/linux/context.cpp
index 461ff26..1a56f00 100644
--- a/core/linux/context.cpp
+++ b/core/linux/context.cpp
@@ -97,7 +97,29 @@ static void context_segfault(host_context_t* hostctx, void* segfault_ctx)
 		#error "Unsupported OS"
 	#endif
 #elif HOST_CPU == CPU_X64
-	#if defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__DragonFly__)
+	#if defined(__ORBIS__)
+		// ⚠ THIS CONSOLE'S SIGNAL CONTEXT LAYOUT IS NOT KNOWN, SO NO REGISTER IS READ HERE.
+		// The SDK ships musl's <bits/signal.h>: a mcontext_t of gregs[23] with REG_RIP and
+		// friends behind #ifdef _GNU_SOURCE, which is not defined on this build - hence the
+		// `use of undeclared identifier 'REG_RIP'` that the __unix__ arm below fails with. The
+		// FreeBSD arm above cannot run either, and not only because the SDK's libc++ __config
+		// does `#undef __FreeBSD__` before this file gets here: that same mcontext_t has no
+		// mc_rip to name.
+		//
+		// So the declared layout is Linux's and the kernel is FreeBSD's, and the port has
+		// already measured that the two do not agree - orbis-compat's crash handler dumps
+		// thirty-two words of this very pointer and reports that NONE of them was a text
+		// address, which is why it falls back to backtrace() for the faulting instruction.
+		// Until that offset is measured and written down, any field named here would be a
+		// guess dressed up as a register.
+		//
+		// The caller in core/linux/common.cpp is patched to match: the fastmem rewrite is the
+		// only thing that reads this context, and it does not run on this platform. A zeroed
+		// host_context_t handed to sh4Dynarec->rewrite() would be worse than no fastmem - it
+		// would patch code at pc 0.
+		(void)hostctx;
+		(void)segfault_ctx;
+	#elif defined(__FreeBSD__) || defined(__DragonFly__)
 		bicopy<ToSegfault>(hostctx->pc, MCTX(.mc_rip));
 		bicopy<ToSegfault>(hostctx->rsp, MCTX(.mc_rsp));
 		bicopy<ToSegfault>(hostctx->r9, MCTX(.mc_r9));
